# AK4619 codec driver options.
#
# This fragment is `rsource`d by the sample that owns the driver and by the
# native_sim suite that exercises it, so the two cannot drift apart. It is
# deliberately outside the AUDIO_PIPELINE_* namespace: issue #42 forbids any
# pipeline symbol from mentioning the codec.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

config AK4619
	bool "AKM AK4619 audio codec"
	default y
	depends on DT_HAS_ASAHI_KASEI_AK4619_ENABLED
	select I2C
	help
	  Driver for the AKM AK4619VN 4-channel audio codec on an I2C control
	  bus. It implements Zephyr's audio_codec API and is built with the
	  sample rather than with the toolkit.

if AK4619

config AK4619_INIT_PRIORITY
	int "AK4619 initialisation priority"
	default 60
	help
	  Device initialisation priority within POST_KERNEL. It must be higher
	  than I2C_INIT_PRIORITY so the control bus exists before the codec
	  tries to use it; the driver asserts that at build time. The default
	  matches Zephyr's own AUDIO_CODEC_INIT_PRIORITY.

config AK4619_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "How long to wait for the codec to answer, in milliseconds"
	default 100
	help
	  After the datasheet's mandatory 10 ms settling time (p.31, p.40 note
	  (3)), the driver polls the control bus for this long before giving up
	  and failing initialisation. The allowance exists because PDN is a
	  manual toggle on the AKD4619-A evaluation board and may be thrown
	  slightly after the host has booted; it is bounded so that an unpowered
	  board fails with a message instead of hanging. Set 0 for a single
	  attempt.

comment "Levels audio_codec_configure() programs"

config AK4619_DAC_VOLUME_HALF_DB
	int "DAC digital volume, in half-decibels"
	default -12
	range -230 24
	help
	  Digital volume written to DAC1 and DAC2, both channels, in 0.5 dB
	  steps: -12 is -6.0 dB, 0 is 0.0 dB, 24 is the part's +12.0 dB
	  maximum (datasheet p.51, Table 22).

	  The default of -6.0 dB is the loop cable's only attenuation, and it
	  is not arbitrary. The DAC's full-scale output and the ADC's
	  single-ended full-scale input are the same number and track the same
	  rail - 2.55/2.83/3.11 Vpp min/typ/max, both 0.858 x AVDD (datasheet
	  p.11 note *14, p.12 note *15) - so a 0 dBFS tone out of the DAC lands
	  exactly at ADC full scale, with part-to-part spread the only margin.
	  Worst case that spread is 1.7 dB the wrong way. 6 dB clears it and
	  still leaves the captured tone ~94 dB above the ADC's noise floor.

	  Changing this changes the level issue #47 measures at the ADC. Record
	  the new pair in docs/hardware/akd4619-evaluation-board.md.

config AK4619_ADC_VOLUME_HALF_DB
	int "ADC digital volume, in half-decibels"
	default 0
	range -206 48
	help
	  Digital volume written to both channels of both ADCs, in 0.5 dB
	  steps from 0 = 0.0 dB (datasheet p.46, Table 14). The default leaves
	  the capture path unity so that what issue #47 measures is the analog
	  loop and not a gain the driver added.

config AK4619_MIC_GAIN_DB
	int "MIC Gain AMP gain, in dB"
	default 0
	range -6 27
	help
	  Gain of the analog amplifier every ADC input passes through
	  (datasheet p.43, Figure 23), in 3 dB steps from -6 to +27 (p.42,
	  Table 9). Must be a multiple of 3; the driver asserts that at build
	  time.

	  0 dB makes it a line input: 2.83 Vpp typ full scale, 25 k input
	  impedance (datasheet p.11), which is what the loop cable carries.
	  Every dB added here is a dB of headroom removed from the loop, and a
	  line-level signal into a stage left near +27 dB clips before the
	  converter ever sees a clean sample.

module = AK4619
module-str = AK4619 audio codec
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # AK4619
